1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,702
You hold a frozen cotton pellet against a tooth, and nothing happens.

2
00:00:03,932 --> 00:00:06,509
No response. So the pulp is necrotic, right?

3
00:00:06,779 --> 00:00:08,289
Well, not always.

4
00:00:08,139 --> 00:00:10,881
About 1 time in 6, the cold test gets it wrong.

5
00:00:11,351 --> 00:00:15,255
That number comes from a 2018 meta-analysis by Mainkar and Kim.

6
00:00:15,405 --> 00:00:18,448
They pooled 28 studies, covering 5 different pulp tests.

7
00:00:18,598 --> 00:00:21,648
And to make sense of the results, you need four ideas that

8
00:00:21,498 --> 00:00:22,980
trip up almost everyone.

9
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:25,391
First, a quick flip in thinking.

10
00:00:25,541 --> 00:00:28,984
In pulp testing, the condition we're hunting for is necrosis.

11
00:00:29,134 --> 00:00:32,413
So a positive test is actually the tooth that doesn't respond.

12
00:00:32,883 --> 00:00:35,375
Imagine 100 teeth we know are necrotic.

13
00:00:35,525 --> 00:00:38,055
Sensitivity asks: how many does the test catch?

14
00:00:37,905 --> 00:00:39,729
For cold, 87.

15
00:00:39,879 --> 00:00:42,237
Now take 100 teeth we know are vital.

16
00:00:42,387 --> 00:00:45,870
Specificity asks: how many respond the way they should?

17
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:46,648
84.

18
00:00:46,798 --> 00:00:49,983
Both of these start with the truth, and then grade the test.

19
00:00:50,453 --> 00:00:52,623
But in the chair, you don't know the truth.

20
00:00:52,473 --> 00:00:53,793
You only see the result.

21
00:00:53,943 --> 00:00:55,572
So the question flips.

22
00:00:55,722 --> 00:00:59,072
If a tooth doesn't respond, what's the chance it's really necrotic?

23
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:00,764
That's the positive predictive value.

24
00:01:00,913 --> 00:01:04,563
And if it does respond, what's the chance it's really vital?

25
00:01:04,413 --> 00:01:06,283
That's the negative predictive value.

26
00:01:06,753 --> 00:01:10,411
For the cold test, those come out to 81 and 87%.

27
00:01:10,561 --> 00:01:13,551
Which means roughly 1 in 5 teeth that don't respond to cold

28
00:01:13,401 --> 00:01:14,880
are actually still vital.

29
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:18,279
The electric pulp test has almost the opposite personality.

30
00:01:18,429 --> 00:01:21,899
Its sensitivity is only 72, so it misses a lot of necrotic

31
00:01:21,749 --> 00:01:22,265
teeth.

32
00:01:22,415 --> 00:01:24,946
But its specificity is 93.

33
00:01:25,096 --> 00:01:28,386
So when the electric test says no response, you can mostly trust

34
00:01:28,236 --> 00:01:31,389
it. Its positive predictive value is 89.

35
00:01:31,859 --> 00:01:35,611
Heat testing comes in last, at 78 and 67.

36
00:01:35,761 --> 00:01:39,811
The clear winners are laser Doppler flowmetry and pulse oximetry, both around

37
00:01:39,661 --> 00:01:41,345
97% accurate.

38
00:01:41,495 --> 00:01:44,745
They look at blood supply, the real marker of a living pulp,

39
00:01:44,595 --> 00:01:46,011
instead of nerve response.

40
00:01:46,481 --> 00:01:47,652
There's one catch.

41
00:01:47,802 --> 00:01:49,732
Predictive values aren't fixed.

42
00:01:49,582 --> 00:01:52,448
They shift with how common necrosis is in the teeth you're testing.

43
00:01:52,598 --> 00:01:55,740
That's why the paper reports adjusted predictive values.

44
00:01:56,210 --> 00:01:57,962
So, here's what to remember.

45
00:01:58,112 --> 00:02:00,317
Cold: 87 and 84.

46
00:02:00,467 --> 00:02:02,919
Electric: 72 and 93.

47
00:02:03,069 --> 00:02:04,470
Heat: last place.

48
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:07,376
And no single test makes the diagnosis on its own.

49
00:02:07,846 --> 00:02:10,516
Save this one for boards, and send it to the co-resident who

50
00:02:10,366 --> 00:02:11,619
still mixes these up.
